new technology has really enabled, er,

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:48.840
this approach, er, i- in analytics and, er,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:57.120
I want to start out now with the notion of the Flaw of Averages.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:04.210
So the flaw of averages, explains, er,

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:13.680
basically why everything [inaudible]

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.910
why so many projects are behind schedule,

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.285
beyond budget, and below the projection.

00:03:20.285 --> 00:03:23.755
And we're just going to start out with- with the,

00:03:23.755 --> 00:03:26.995
er, behind schedule part.

00:03:26.995 --> 00:03:32.615
So suppose the project involves creating a new website and

00:03:32.615 --> 00:03:35.150
it consists of 10 pages without

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:40.050
the shopping cart page and the legal page and the credit card page whatever.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:44.750
Naturally, we're uncertain about how long each page will take to complete but

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:50.220
they- they are being created in parallel,

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.190
uh, by 10 separate teams.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.610
So this is the best of all possible projects.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.740
Team 1 does Page 1,

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.585
Team 2 does Page 2.

00:04:00.585 --> 00:04:02.790
When we get all 10 pages done,

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.605
we can launch, uh, the website.

00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:08.570
But of course we're uncertain about how long each team will take.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:12.485
So on average, they take six weeks but who,

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.270
you know, that could happen or that could happen or that could

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.105
happen but on average there six weeks.

00:04:17.105 --> 00:04:20.775
So here's- here is where the flaw of averages comes in.

00:04:20.775 --> 00:04:23.315
The boss comes in and says, ''Hey,

00:04:23.315 --> 00:04:26.340
when's the website can be up,'' and you say,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.000
''I don't know boss because I don't know how page- long page one will take

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.795
a Page 2 or Page 3 and then the boss says, ''Correct me if I'm wrong.

00:04:32.795 --> 00:04:35.180
Give me a number.''

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.870
Now give me a number is actually the fork in the road

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.000
to hell because after you've done that,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.950
you really can't do anymore analysis if you give them a number but you say to the boss,

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:48.415
''I can't give you a number but I can give you an average.''

00:04:48.415 --> 00:04:54.360
Because on average, each of these is six weeks.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.885
So on average, this project will be done in six weeks,

00:04:57.885 --> 00:05:00.430
and the boss says well if I can give you an average

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.475
okay and you've told me six weeks, so that's what we're thinking about.

00:05:03.475 --> 00:05:07.500
Now, um, I'd like to ask you a question here,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.785
you don't have to answer it on the poll because we've already done this poll.

00:05:10.785 --> 00:05:12.600
Just think for a second,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:18.620
what is the chance that this project will actually be done in six weeks?

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:22.065
A hundred percent, 50 percent, 1 in 4,

00:05:22.065 --> 00:05:29.790
1 in 10,1 in 100,1 in 1,000,1 in 10,000 let it sink for just a bit on that.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.620
Last year we did a poll on this,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.910
so I know the answers.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:36.780
I know what you think the answers are. Okay, here it goes.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.255
Most people think, uh,

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:43.125
50-50 chance would be done in six weeks.

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:46.690
The correct answer is 1 in 1,000.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:51.350
And it's not enough to just understand that

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:55.880
but you've got to be able to explain it to your boss or say a 10 year-old.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.360
Okay, so let me explain why it's 1 in 1,000.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.289
Imagine that each of these ten pages,

00:06:04.289 --> 00:06:06.625
the duration of each of the ten pages [NOISE]- is

00:06:06.625 --> 00:06:12.225
either over six weeks or under six weeks determined by a coin toss.

00:06:12.225 --> 00:06:20.080
Well the only way you can go live in six weeks is if all 10 of these

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.680
coming in at six weeks or under and

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:27.580
that's like flipping 10 heads in a row which is actually 1,000 and,

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:31.165
er, er, one chance in 1,124.

00:06:31.165 --> 00:06:33.265
So that's why, er,

00:06:33.265 --> 00:06:36.730
the answer here is about 1 in 1,000.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:40.955
Now, er, I think it's important

00:06:40.955 --> 00:06:46.930
to point out that there been some technological breakthroughs ,uh,

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:52.730
that have allowed, uh,

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:56.780
enabled a new discipline that I call Probability Management.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:02.625
And Probability Management makes

00:07:02.625 --> 00:07:10.270
uncertainty actionable by storing it as auditable data.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:14.080
As an example-

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.860
Let's- let's do dice here.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.510
