00:10:47.275 --> 00:10:51.040
So now let's go back to the scheduling problem.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:54.565
First thing to say is gee,

00:10:54.565 --> 00:10:56.635
how do you define the flaw of averages?

00:10:56.635 --> 00:11:01.840
While the flaw of averages states that plans based on

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:07.240
average assumptions are wrong on average.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.390
So, uh, for example, oh yeah,

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:14.560
most people think this will be done in six weeks in 50- 50-50 chance.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.345
Right, there's one chance with 1,000,

00:11:16.345 --> 00:11:18.970
that's not close enough even for government work.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:22.405
Um, so- so here's one example of the flaw of averages.

00:11:22.405 --> 00:11:28.045
Uh, consider a statistician who drowned in the river that's on average three feet deep.

00:11:28.045 --> 00:11:30.310
A- again this is another, uh,

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:33.325
cartoon by Jeff Danziger who's illustrated the book.

00:11:33.325 --> 00:11:37.990
Um, he- here's a more sobering example.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:41.350
Consider a drunk wandering back and forth in a busy highway.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:46.345
Ah, his average position is the center line.

00:11:46.345 --> 00:11:50.440
Well, that means that at his average position, the drunk is alive.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:52.390
Yeah, but on average,

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:56.560
he's dead not close enough even for government work.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:58.780
Now, this- this was discovered.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:03.310
This is a mathematical principle known as Jensen's inequality.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:11.900
And, um, just one of the other points here.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:16.850
Um, i- in the Flaw of Averages book, um,

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:21.665
one of the big problems here i- in statistics,

00:12:21.665 --> 00:12:24.840
ah, is that most people had statistics course.

